6.2  What happens when you open a website

TL;DR
  - DNS: the name becomes an IP address (15.2).
  - TCP: a three-way handshake opens a connection to port 443.
  - TLS: client and server agree on encryption keys, and the server proves who
    it is with a certificate.
  - HTTP: the browser sends a request, the server answers. With HTTP/3, the
    transport is QUIC, over UDP, instead of TCP.

      curl -v -o /dev/null https://example.com     # every step, in order

On your machine
  L2 link   wlp0s20f3 up · neighbour 192.168.1.1 is f4:92:bf:0e:21:7a on
            wlp0s20f3
  L3 IP     docker0 172.17.0.1/16, wlp0s20f3 192.168.1.23/24, wlp0s20f3
            fe80::8ec6:81ff:fe2a:107f/64 · gateway 192.168.1.1 · forwards
            packets for others
  L4 TCP    12 connections open now; 30,120 started and 410 accepted since boot
            · 1,840 of 3.1 million segments resent
  L4 UDP    95,000 datagrams in, 61,000 out, since boot
  L7 DNS    127.0.0.53

Pitfalls
  - A slow first request is often DNS or the TLS handshake.
    curl -w '%{time_namelookup} %{time_connect} %{time_appconnect}\n' splits the
    time between them.
  - A certificate error means a name or date that doesn't match, or an issuer
    that isn't trusted: check the machine's clock first.
  - A proxy (HTTPS_PROXY) changes the whole path: curl -v shows it.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 6.2 --why

